  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nTemperatures are forecast to be near to slightly above normal \nthrough the week and into the weekend. Winds generally below 15 \nknots varying from north to east can be expected through the work \nweek, before becoming southeast over the weekend.\n\nRain chances will be very low today due to drier air moving across S \nTX. Rain chances increase across the coastal waters this afternoon \nwith the activity moving inland overnight. This activity is \nassociated with a surface to mid level trough moving west and \nbringing deep gulf moisture (PWAT 1.9 inches) to the area. There is \na 20-40% chance of mainly showers along the coastline. As the trough \nmoves farther west, the chance of showers shifts west across the \nsouthern Coastal Plains and southern Brush Country through the day. \nRain chances diminish Wednesday night with very low to nil rain \nchances through the remainder of the week as mid/upper level high \npressure takes hold. Models indicate low rain chances return Sunday \nand increase early next week.\n\nA northeast to east wind will persist across the gulf waters through \nat least Thursday, if not Friday, due to the series of surface to \nmid level troughs moving west across the region. Although the flow \nwill generally be 5-15 knots, the persistent direction and swell \nperiods around 8 seconds could lead to minor coastal flooding during \ntimes of high tide. Latest P-ETSS run indicated that tide levels may \nexceed the 2ft MSL threshold with the high tide Tuesday night/ \nWednesday morning. Confidence is low to moderate. Trends will \ncontinue to be monitored for possible Coastal Flood Advisories.\n\nWill continue with a moderate rip current risk today and Wednesday \ndue to swell periods around 8 seconds. The northeast flow tends to \nlower the rip current risk, but 8 second period swells tends to \nincrease the risk.",
